claimed: intent-label-distillation — reprioritised by my own learning curve and scoped to what it supports: NOT the probe, whose curve is flat past 32 examples, but the ZERO-NETWORK tiers, chargrams (30 -> 65% and still climbing) and the static table (63.3%), which are starved rather than finished and are the only candidates for a classifier that needs no network at all. Method: the model writes messages per class in batches, a second pass classifies them back with the shipped prompt, and only the ones where generation and classification AGREE survive -- self-consistency as the filter, since a label the model contradicts on sight is not worth training on. Evaluation stays on the human-labelled fixture and never touches generated data, so the number cannot be inflated by the corpus that produced it. What this can show: whether the no-network path moves toward the 86.7% the embedding tiers reach, or whether 63% is where a bag of units stops regardless of how much it is fed.
